{"subjectId":"c","id":"void-pointers","title":"void Pointers & Generic Code","group":"Pointers","order":28,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>void *</code> is a pointer to <em>something</em> of unknown type. Any object pointer converts to and from it implicitly — it is how C writes generic code (<code>malloc</code>, <code>memcpy</code>, <code>qsort</code>).</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid print_value(const void *p, char type) {\n    switch (type) {\n        case 'i': printf(\"int %d\\n\", *(const int *)p); break;\n        case 'd': printf(\"double %.2f\\n\", *(const double *)p); break;\n        case 's': printf(\"string %s\\n\", (const char *)p); break;\n    }\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int i = 7;\n    double d = 2.5;\n    print_value(&amp;i, 'i');\n    print_value(&amp;d, 'd');\n    print_value(\"text\", 's');\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>int 7\ndouble 2.50\nstring text</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Rules</h3>\n<ul><li>You <strong>cannot dereference</strong> a <code>void *</code> — cast it to a concrete type first.</li><li>No pointer arithmetic on <code>void *</code> in standard C (GCC allows it as an extension, treating the size as 1).</li><li><code>T *</code> → <code>void *</code> → <code>T *</code> round-trips safely. Converting to the <em>wrong</em> type and dereferencing is undefined behaviour.</li><li>In C, <code>malloc</code>’s <code>void *</code> result needs no cast: <code>int *p = malloc(n * sizeof *p);</code>. (C++ requires one.)</li><li>Function pointers are <strong>not</strong> guaranteed to convert to <code>void *</code> (POSIX allows it for <code>dlsym</code>).</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-exchange\"></i>A generic swap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\nvoid swap_any(void *a, void *b, size_t size) {\n    unsigned char tmp[64];            /* enough for this demo */\n    if (size &gt; sizeof tmp) return;\n    memcpy(tmp, a, size);\n    memcpy(a, b, size);\n    memcpy(b, tmp, size);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    double p = 1.5, q = 9.75;\n    swap_any(&amp;x, &amp;y, sizeof x);\n    swap_any(&amp;p, &amp;q, sizeof p);\n    printf(\"%d %d %.2f %.2f\\n\", x, y, p, q);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 1 9.75 1.50</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>A generic linear search</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;string.h&gt;\n\n/* returns the index of the first element equal to key, or -1 */\nlong find(const void *base, size_t n, size_t size, const void *key,\n          int (*eq)(const void *, const void *)) {\n    const unsigned char *p = base;             /* byte pointer for arithmetic */\n    for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)\n        if (eq(p + i * size, key)) return (long)i;\n    return -1;\n}\n\nint eq_int(const void *a, const void *b) { return *(const int *)a == *(const int *)b; }\nint eq_str(const void *a, const void *b) {\n    return strcmp(*(const char *const *)a, *(const char *const *)b) == 0;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int nums[] = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};\n    int k = 4;\n    const char *names[] = {\"ann\", \"bob\", \"cy\"};\n    const char *who = \"cy\";\n    printf(\"%ld %ld\\n\", find(nums, 5, sizeof nums[0], &amp;k, eq_int),\n           find(names, 3, sizeof names[0], &amp;who, eq_str));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>void *</code> throws away type checking — the compiler can no longer catch a wrong cast. Keep the <code>void *</code> boundary thin and convert back to real types immediately.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't a void pointer be dereferenced?</summary><div><p>Dereferencing needs to know the size and interpretation of the object. <code>void</code> is an incomplete type with no size, so the compiler cannot generate a load or store — you must first cast to a complete type.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does qsort's comparator take const void *?</summary><div><p><code>qsort</code> is written once for every element type, so it only knows the base address, the element count and the element size. It hands the comparator pointers to two elements as <code>const void *</code>; the comparator, which knows the real type, casts them back.</p></div></details></section>"}